WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.374
A bank refills the cash machines of two branches, Nizami and Khatai, from

00:04.474 --> 00:08.541
a single cash centre. Does a heavy day of withdrawals in Nizami make

00:08.641 --> 00:12.464
a heavy day in Khatai more likely? If not, the treasury can plan

00:12.564 --> 00:16.387
each branch from its own history. If so, both running dry on the

00:16.487 --> 00:18.838
same day is a bigger risk than it looks.

00:20.933 --> 00:24.005
Independence has a simple picture: learning one variable

00:24.105 --> 00:26.214
does not change the shape of the other.

00:27.300 --> 00:31.684
Here is one possible joint table for the two branches, each having

00:31.784 --> 00:35.421
a low, medium or heavy day. Next to every row, draw the

00:35.521 --> 00:39.973
conditional distribution of Khatai: the row, rescaled to total one.

00:40.066 --> 00:43.588
All three profiles are identical. Whatever happened in Nizami,

00:43.688 --> 00:47.092
Khatai's chances stay sixty, thirty, ten. Knowing one branch

00:47.192 --> 00:50.363
tells you nothing about the other. That is independence.

00:50.450 --> 00:53.965
Now a second table with exactly the same row and column

00:54.065 --> 00:58.171
totals, but a different inside. The profiles tilt: after a heavy

00:58.271 --> 01:01.458
day in Nizami, a heavy day in Khatai is five times

01:01.558 --> 01:05.468
as likely as after a quiet one. These branches are dependent,

01:05.568 --> 01:08.557
perhaps because salaries and holidays hit both.

01:09.650 --> 01:14.772
To test independence without drawing profiles, use the margins. Independence means

01:14.872 --> 01:18.274
every cell equals its row total times its column total.

01:19.366 --> 01:23.469
Check the first cell. Zero point four, against zero point five

01:23.569 --> 01:27.604
times zero point six, which is zero point three. They differ,

01:27.704 --> 01:32.146
and that single cell is enough: the branches are dependent. Proving

01:32.246 --> 01:35.331
independence would need all nine cells to pass.

01:35.416 --> 01:38.284
And it matters for the cash van. Both branches heavy

01:38.384 --> 01:41.308
on the same day has probability five percent, not the

01:41.408 --> 01:45.417
two percent that multiplying would give. Planning each branch separately

01:45.517 --> 01:47.986
would understate that risk by more than half.

01:49.083 --> 01:53.175
For densities, the picture is the same: slice at a value of

01:53.275 --> 01:57.225
y one, and ask whether the slice has the same shape every

01:57.325 --> 02:01.417
time. Compare a density on a square with one on a triangle.

02:01.516 --> 02:05.116
Sweep y one from left to right. On the square, every slice

02:05.216 --> 02:09.836
gives the same conditional curve, three y two squared: independent. On the

02:09.936 --> 02:14.045
triangle, the slice gets longer as y one grows, so the conditional

02:14.145 --> 02:18.255
density keeps changing width. Knowing y one tells you the range of

02:18.355 --> 02:22.210
y two, so they are dependent, whatever the formula looks like.

02:22.300 --> 02:26.459
That gives the quick test for densities. Independence holds exactly

02:26.559 --> 02:29.511
when the density splits into a function of y one

02:29.611 --> 02:32.309
times a function of y two, over a rectangle.

02:33.400 --> 02:37.587
So: two variables are independent when every conditional slice has the

02:37.687 --> 02:40.833
same shape. In a table, every cell must equal its row

02:40.933 --> 02:44.997
total times its column total, and one failure proves dependence. For

02:45.097 --> 02:47.876
a density, look for a product over a rectangle.
